.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Benevento","recordId":"t63-87ec311b1824","supportScope":"Saved chronological reference; not newly fetched"},{"file":"Existing Tapestry historical record","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teutonic_Order","recordId":"t63-88ffd2ea6687","supportScope":"Saved chronological reference; not newly fetched"}],"explanation74":"Teutonic Order founded (1190 CE); Battle of Benevento (1266 CE); Outrage of Anagni (1303 CE).","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0537","nodeId":"FACT101-ARCHED-NEW101-0537","anchor":"MAJOR:HISTORY-AND-POLITICS","subject":"Place these events in chronological order, from earliest to latest.\nA. Battle of Chaeronea\nB. Battle of Cannae\nC. Death of Alexander the Great","value":"A → C → B","group":"Historical sequence","major":"History & Politics","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"Existing Tapestry historical record","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Chaeronea_(338_BC)","recordId":"t63-9543d1eff267","supportScope":"Saved chronological reference; not newly fetched"},{"file":"Existing Tapestry historical record","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Cannae","recordId":"t63-7b27e105b1f4","supportScope":"Saved chronological reference; not newly fetched"},{"file":"Existing Tapestry historical record","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_the_Great","recordId":"t63-a9c56288b462","supportScope":"Saved chronological reference; not newly fetched"}],"explanation74":"Battle of Chaeronea (338 BCE); Death of Alexander the Great (323 BCE); Battle of Cannae (216 BCE).","previewOnly95":false,"review101":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-001","nodeId":"FACT106-001","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Angkor was a major centre of which civilization?","value":"Khmer","group":"Angkor","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/668/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Angkor combines temples, reservoirs and urban infrastructure. Angkor Wat and the Bayon belong to different building campaigns; the long range describes the city, not one temple.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-002","nodeId":"FACT106-002","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What makes reservoirs important to understanding Angkor?","value":"They connect temples with water management and urban infrastructure","group":"Angkor","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/668/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Angkor combines temples, reservoirs and urban infrastructure. Angkor Wat and the Bayon belong to different building campaigns; the long range describes the city, not one temple.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-003","nodeId":"FACT106-003","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What was the original purpose of the Taj Mahal?","value":"A mausoleum for Mumtaz Mahal","group":"Taj Mahal","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/252/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Shah Jahan commissioned this mausoleum for Mumtaz Mahal. Read the white marble tomb together with its garden and symmetrical complex; it is a funerary monument, not a royal residence.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-004","nodeId":"FACT106-004","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which ruler commissioned the Taj Mahal?","value":"Shah Jahan","group":"Taj Mahal","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/252/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Shah Jahan commissioned this mausoleum for Mumtaz Mahal. Read the white marble tomb together with its garden and symmetrical complex; it is a funerary monument, not a royal residence.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-005","nodeId":"FACT106-005","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which religious tradition is central to Borobudur?","value":"Buddhism","group":"Borobudur","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"A Buddhist monument with square terraces below circular platforms and a crowning stupa. The ascending route organizes reliefs and repeated stupas into a spatial sequence.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-006","nodeId":"FACT106-006","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which spatial sequence characterizes Borobudur?","value":"Square terraces below circular platforms and a stupa","group":"Borobudur","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"A Buddhist monument with square terraces below circular platforms and a crowning stupa. The ascending route organizes reliefs and repeated stupas into a spatial sequence.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-007","nodeId":"FACT106-007","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Petra is especially associated with which ancient people?","value":"Nabataeans","group":"Petra","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/326/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Nabataean Petra combined caravan commerce, rock-cut architecture and careful water management. Carved facades do not mean that every part of the city was cut into rock.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-008","nodeId":"FACT106-008","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which pair best explains how Petra functioned as a desert city?","value":"Caravan commerce and water management","group":"Petra","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/326/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Nabataean Petra combined caravan commerce, rock-cut architecture and careful water management. Carved facades do not mean that every part of the city was cut into rock.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-009","nodeId":"FACT106-009","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Machu Picchu belongs to which civilization?","value":"Inca","group":"Machu Picchu","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/274/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The Inca site joins stone buildings, terraces and steep mountain terrain. Its fifteenth-century setting distinguishes it from earlier Andean cultures such as the Moche.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-010","nodeId":"FACT106-010","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which description best fits the setting of Machu Picchu?","value":"Stone buildings and terraces integrated with steep Andean terrain","group":"Machu Picchu","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/274/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The Inca site joins stone buildings, terraces and steep mountain terrain. Its fifteenth-century setting distinguishes it from earlier Andean cultures such as the Moche.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-011","nodeId":"FACT106-011","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Chichén Itzá is located in which cultural-geographic setting?","value":"The Maya region of Yucatán","group":"Chichén Itzá","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/483/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Temple pyramids, a large ball court and other monuments show a Maya city with wider Mesoamerican connections. Do not confuse it with Teotihuacan in central Mexico.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-012","nodeId":"FACT106-012","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which ensemble is associated with Chichén Itzá?","value":"Temple pyramids and a large ball court","group":"Chichén Itzá","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/483/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Temple pyramids, a large ball court and other monuments show a Maya city with wider Mesoamerican connections. Do not confuse it with Teotihuacan in central Mexico.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-013","nodeId":"FACT106-013","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which statement correctly places Teotihuacan in time?","value":"It flourished centuries before the Aztec Empire","group":"Teotihuacan","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/414/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The Avenue of the Dead links monumental precincts including the Sun and Moon pyramids. The city flourished many centuries before the Aztec Empire; its builders should not be labelled Aztec.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-014","nodeId":"FACT106-014","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which major route organizes the monumental core of Teotihuacan?","value":"Avenue of the Dead","group":"Teotihuacan","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/414/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The Avenue of the Dead links monumental precincts including the Sun and Moon pyramids. The city flourished many centuries before the Aztec Empire; its builders should not be labelled Aztec.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-015","nodeId":"FACT106-015","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"The moai of Rapa Nui belong to which broad cultural world?","value":"Polynesia","group":"Rapa Nui: moai","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/715/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The moai are monumental stone figures associated with a Polynesian island society. Many stand on ceremonial platforms called ahu. The range is a broad period, not a date for every statue.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-016","nodeId":"FACT106-016","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What is an ahu in the context of Rapa Nui?","value":"A ceremonial platform associated with moai","group":"Rapa Nui: moai","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/715/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The moai are monumental stone figures associated with a Polynesian island society. Many stand on ceremonial platforms called ahu. The range is a broad period, not a date for every statue.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-017","nodeId":"FACT106-017","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which structural material is fundamental to Himeji Castle?","value":"Timber","group":"Himeji Castle","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/661/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"White plastered surfaces cover a sophisticated timber castle. Gates, walls and routes form a defensive system: its appearance is not simply ornamental.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-018","nodeId":"FACT106-018","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Why should Himeji be read as more than an ornamental white building?","value":"Its gates, walls and routes form a defensive system","group":"Himeji Castle","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/661/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"White plastered surfaces cover a sophisticated timber castle. Gates, walls and routes form a defensive system: its appearance is not simply ornamental.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-019","nodeId":"FACT106-019","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which dynasties used Beijing’s Forbidden City as an imperial palace?","value":"Ming and Qing","group":"Forbidden City","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/439/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Beijing's imperial palace developed as a hierarchy of courts and halls along an axis. The initial Ming construction dates do not imply that every surviving structure is original.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-020","nodeId":"FACT106-020","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which principle helps explain the plan of the Forbidden City?","value":"An axial hierarchy of courtyards and halls","group":"Forbidden City","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/439/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Beijing's imperial palace developed as a hierarchy of courts and halls along an axis. The initial Ming construction dates do not imply that every surviving structure is original.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-021","nodeId":"FACT106-021","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What was the Temple of Heaven primarily designed for?","value":"Imperial ceremonies relating heaven and earth","group":"Temple of Heaven","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/881/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"This ceremonial complex relates imperial ritual to ideas of heaven and earth. Read its symbolism and layout as a religious-political landscape rather than a residential palace.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-022","nodeId":"FACT106-022","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which pairing correctly distinguishes two Beijing complexes?","value":"Forbidden City: imperial palace; Temple of Heaven: ritual complex","group":"Temple of Heaven","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/881/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"This ceremonial complex relates imperial ritual to ideas of heaven and earth. Read its symbolism and layout as a religious-political landscape rather than a residential palace.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-023","nodeId":"FACT106-023","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Potala Palace served as a winter residence of whom?","value":"The Dalai Lama","group":"Potala Palace","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/707/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The palace was a winter residence of the Dalai Lama and a centre of Tibetan Buddhist authority. Its major seventeenth-century development stands on a site with earlier history.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-024","nodeId":"FACT106-024","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which cultural setting is essential to understanding Potala Palace?","value":"Tibetan Buddhism","group":"Potala Palace","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/707/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The palace was a winter residence of the Dalai Lama and a centre of Tibetan Buddhist authority. Its major seventeenth-century development stands on a site with earlier history.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-025","nodeId":"FACT106-025","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"How were the celebrated medieval churches at Lalibela formed?","value":"By carving spaces and structures from rock","group":"Lalibela: rock-hewn churches","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/18/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Eleven medieval churches were carved from rock and connected by trenches and passages. Excavating spaces and leaving structural masses produces architecture by subtraction.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-026","nodeId":"FACT106-026","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which religious tradition is represented by Lalibela’s rock-hewn churches?","value":"Ethiopian Christianity","group":"Lalibela: rock-hewn churches","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/18/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Eleven medieval churches were carved from rock and connected by trenches and passages. Excavating spaces and leaving structural masses produces architecture by subtraction.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-027","nodeId":"FACT106-027","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Great Zimbabwe is associated with which historical cultural context?","value":"Shona society and regional trade","group":"Great Zimbabwe","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The ruins record a Shona urban and trading centre. Carefully fitted granite walls, especially the Great Enclosure, challenge the idea that monumental African architecture was limited to Egypt.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-028","nodeId":"FACT106-028","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which material is characteristic of the monumental walls at Great Zimbabwe?","value":"Granite","group":"Great Zimbabwe","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/364/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The ruins record a Shona urban and trading centre. Carefully fitted granite walls, especially the Great Enclosure, challenge the idea that monumental African architecture was limited to Egypt.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-029","nodeId":"FACT106-029","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which material tradition is central to the historic architecture of Djenné?","value":"Earthen construction","group":"Djenné: historic town","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/116/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Earthen construction links the town's buildings to local materials and continuing maintenance. The range describes a historic urban phase, not the construction date of today's Great Mosque.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-030","nodeId":"FACT106-030","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Why must the history of Djenné be distinguished from the date of its present Great Mosque?","value":"A town’s long history is not the construction date of each surviving building","group":"Djenné: historic town","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/116/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Earthen construction links the town's buildings to local materials and continuing maintenance. The range describes a historic urban phase, not the construction date of today's Great Mosque.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-031","nodeId":"FACT106-031","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Persepolis was a monumental centre of which empire?","value":"Achaemenid Persian Empire","group":"Persepolis","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/114/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Darius I began the Achaemenid palace complex on a monumental terrace. Reliefs and ceremonial spaces expressed imperial authority; this is not a Greek polis.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-032","nodeId":"FACT106-032","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which ruler initiated the building of Persepolis around 518 BCE?","value":"Darius I","group":"Persepolis","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/114/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Darius I began the Achaemenid palace complex on a monumental terrace. Reliefs and ceremonial spaces expressed imperial authority; this is not a Greek polis.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-033","nodeId":"FACT106-033","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"In the context of Sanchi, what is a stupa?","value":"A Buddhist sacred mound or reliquary monument","group":"Sanchi","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/524/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Stupas, gateways and other remains trace a long Buddhist history beginning in the Mauryan period. The stupa is a sacred mound or reliquary monument, not an ordinary dwelling.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-034","nodeId":"FACT106-034","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"The early Buddhist monuments at Sanchi reach back to which period?","value":"The Mauryan period","group":"Sanchi","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/524/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Stupas, gateways and other remains trace a long Buddhist history beginning in the Mauryan period. The stupa is a sacred mound or reliquary monument, not an ordinary dwelling.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-035","nodeId":"FACT106-035","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which religious traditions are represented among the temples at Khajuraho?","value":"Hinduism and Jainism","group":"Khajuraho","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/240/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The surviving temples combine towered sanctuaries with extensive sculpture. Both Hindu and Jain monuments are represented; reducing the site to erotic imagery misses its wider religious programme.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-036","nodeId":"FACT106-036","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which dynasty is associated with the main temple-building phase at Khajuraho?","value":"Chandella","group":"Khajuraho","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/240/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The surviving temples combine towered sanctuaries with extensive sculpture. Both Hindu and Jain monuments are represented; reducing the site to erotic imagery misses its wider religious programme.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-037","nodeId":"FACT106-037","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Bagan’s monument landscape is primarily associated with which religion?","value":"Buddhism","group":"Bagan","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1588/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Thousands of Buddhist monuments form a landscape shaped by patronage and merit-making. The selected range describes the principal building era, not every restoration.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-038","nodeId":"FACT106-038","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which activity helped shape Bagan’s dense landscape of temples?","value":"Religious patronage and merit-making","group":"Bagan","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1588/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Thousands of Buddhist monuments form a landscape shaped by patronage and merit-making. The selected range describes the principal building era, not every restoration.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-039","nodeId":"FACT106-039","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Samarkand’s major fourteenth- and fifteenth-century monuments are especially associated with which dynasty?","value":"Timurid","group":"Samarkand","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/603/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Samarkand links Silk Road exchange with Timurid monuments and scholarship. The Registan, Bibi-Khanum Mosque and Ulugh Beg's observatory have different functions and building histories.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-040","nodeId":"FACT106-040","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What was the function of Ulugh Beg’s observatory at Samarkand?","value":"Astronomical observation","group":"Samarkand","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/603/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Samarkand links Silk Road exchange with Timurid monuments and scholarship. The Registan, Bibi-Khanum Mosque and Ulugh Beg's observatory have different functions and building histories.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-041","nodeId":"FACT106-041","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Hampi was the capital of which southern Indian kingdom?","value":"Vijayanagara","group":"Hampi","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/241/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The former Vijayanagara capital brings together temples, royal structures and a dramatic rocky landscape. The ruins belong to a major southern Indian kingdom, not to Mughal Agra.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-042","nodeId":"FACT106-042","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which pairing correctly locates Hampi and the Taj Mahal in different historical settings?","value":"Hampi: Vijayanagara capital; Taj Mahal: Mughal mausoleum","group":"Hampi","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/241/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The former Vijayanagara capital brings together temples, royal structures and a dramatic rocky landscape. The ruins belong to a major southern Indian kingdom, not to Mughal Agra.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-043","nodeId":"FACT106-043","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"The Sun Temple at Konark represents the chariot of which deity?","value":"Surya","group":"Konark Sun Temple","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/246/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The temple gives architectural form to the chariot of Surya, the sun god. Carved wheels and horses turn an entire building into a religious image.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-044","nodeId":"FACT106-044","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which carved elements make the chariot symbolism of Konark legible?","value":"Wheels and horses","group":"Konark Sun Temple","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/246/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The temple gives architectural form to the chariot of Surya, the sun god. Carved wheels and horses turn an entire building into a religious image.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-045","nodeId":"FACT106-045","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Mogao’s location near Dunhuang connects its Buddhist art to which exchange network?","value":"The Silk Road","group":"Mogao Caves","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/440/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Wall paintings and sculpture preserve many centuries of Buddhist art along the Silk Road. The caves also connect art history with manuscripts and intercultural exchange.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-046","nodeId":"FACT106-046","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which forms of art are particularly associated with the Mogao Caves?","value":"Buddhist wall paintings and sculpture","group":"Mogao Caves","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/440/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Wall paintings and sculpture preserve many centuries of Buddhist art along the Silk Road. The caves also connect art history with manuscripts and intercultural exchange.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-047","nodeId":"FACT106-047","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"Which dynastic periods are especially important for Longmen’s Buddhist sculpture?","value":"Northern Wei and Tang","group":"Longmen Grottoes","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1003/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Rock-cut Buddhist images reveal changing styles and patronage, especially under Northern Wei and Tang rule. Compare carved stone at Longmen with painted cave interiors at Mogao.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-048","nodeId":"FACT106-048","anchor":"MAJOR:ARCHITECTURE","subject":"What is the principal artistic medium of the monumental Buddhist images at Longmen?","value":"Rock-cut stone sculpture","group":"Longmen Grottoes","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"UNESCO World Heritage Centre","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1003/","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Rock-cut Buddhist images reveal changing styles and patronage, especially under Northern Wei and Tang rule. Compare carved stone at Longmen with painted cave interiors at Mogao.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-049","nodeId":"FACT106-049","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which technique was used for Hokusai’s Great Wave?","value":"Woodblock printing","group":"The Great Wave off Kanagawa","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/56353","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Look for tiny Mount Fuji beneath the crest and the three boats. This is a Japanese woodblock print from Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji, not an oil painting; size and framing make the mountain seem vulnerable.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-050","nodeId":"FACT106-050","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which mountain appears beneath the wave’s crest in this print?","value":"Mount Fuji","group":"The Great Wave off Kanagawa","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/56353","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Look for tiny Mount Fuji beneath the crest and the three boats. This is a Japanese woodblock print from Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji, not an oil painting; size and framing make the mountain seem vulnerable.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-051","nodeId":"FACT106-051","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which deity is represented here as Lord of Dance?","value":"Shiva","group":"Shiva as Lord of Dance (Nataraja)","major":"Art History","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39329","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The dancing Shiva combines movement with balance. A drum evokes creation and a flame destruction; the figure underfoot represents illusion. The copper-alloy sculpture belongs to a Hindu context, unlike a Buddha image.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-052","nodeId":"FACT106-052","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"In this Nataraja image, what do drum and flame evoke?","value":"Creation and destruction","group":"Shiva as Lord of Dance (Nataraja)","major":"Art History","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39329","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The dancing Shiva combines movement with balance. A drum evokes creation and a flame destruction; the figure underfoot represents illusion. The copper-alloy sculpture belongs to a Hindu context, unlike a Buddha image.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-053","nodeId":"FACT106-053","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"What does the relative scale of the king and attendants communicate in this Benin plaque?","value":"Hierarchy of rank","group":"Plaque with equestrian oba and attendants","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/310752","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Compare the scale of the king with his attendants: relative size communicates rank. This is cast brass court art from Benin in present-day Nigeria. The wider tradition was dispersed through the British looting of the palace in 1897; provenance is part of its history.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-054","nodeId":"FACT106-054","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"What is the material of this court plaque from Benin?","value":"Brass","group":"Plaque with equestrian oba and attendants","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/310752","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Compare the scale of the king with his attendants: relative size communicates rank. This is cast brass court art from Benin in present-day Nigeria. The wider tradition was dispersed through the British looting of the palace in 1897; provenance is part of its history.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-055","nodeId":"FACT106-055","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"This Buddha is dated 680. Which Chinese dynasty provides its historical context?","value":"Tang","group":"Buddha, dated 680","major":"Art History","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39756","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"This marble Buddha is explicitly dated 680 in the collection record. Separate the date of this object from the much longer Tang dynasty (618–907); a dynasty is context, not an exact date for every work.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-056","nodeId":"FACT106-056","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which date belongs to this particular Buddha, rather than to the whole dynasty?","value":"680","group":"Buddha, dated 680","major":"Art History","era":"Medieval","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/39756","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"This marble Buddha is explicitly dated 680 in the collection record. Separate the date of this object from the much longer Tang dynasty (618–907); a dynasty is context, not an exact date for every work.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-057","nodeId":"FACT106-057","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which material and decorative process describe this Iznik-attributed bowl?","value":"Stonepaste with blue painting under transparent glaze","group":"Bowl with Variation of 'Baba Nakkas' Design","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/448664","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Blue and white floral decoration lies beneath a transparent glaze on stonepaste. The attribution is to Iznik; the design-family name does not securely identify the individual potter. Notice how ornament follows the curved vessel.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-058","nodeId":"FACT106-058","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Why is it misleading to treat “Baba Nakkas” as a securely identified maker of this bowl?","value":"The name describes a design group, not a secure individual attribution","group":"Bowl with Variation of 'Baba Nakkas' Design","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/448664","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"Blue and white floral decoration lies beneath a transparent glaze on stonepaste. The attribution is to Iznik; the design-family name does not securely identify the individual potter. Notice how ornament follows the curved vessel.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-059","nodeId":"FACT106-059","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which culture produced this portrait-head vessel?","value":"Moche","group":"Stirrup Spout Bottle with Portrait Head","major":"Art History","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/308531","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The portrait head is also a ceramic vessel, with a stirrup-shaped spout joining its passages. The Moche context predates the Inca Empire by centuries; shared geography does not mean a single culture or period.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"},{"id":"FACT106-060","nodeId":"FACT106-060","anchor":"MAJOR:ART-HISTORY","subject":"Which statement correctly relates this Moche vessel to Inca history?","value":"It predates the Inca Empire by centuries","group":"Stirrup Spout Bottle with Portrait Head","major":"Art History","era":"Ancient","relation":"review question","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/308531","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Object or site identity, historical context and the tested relation"}],"explanation74":"The portrait head is also a ceramic vessel, with a stirrup-shaped spout joining its passages. The Moche context predates the Inca Empire by centuries; shared geography does not mean a single culture or period.","previewOnly95":false,"review106":"editorially_reviewed"}]